/-
Fmt/MustBeRefused/M1FilesB.lean: every proof in this file is false, or breaks a rule of the
protocol, and must be refused. These are the 31 files of the set in Fmt/M1FilesB.lean that a
cheating prover has made, as bytes, each with its expected refusal beside it.
This file holds the bytes of these files written out as Lean literals; it was made by a program and
is not meant to be read.

Definitions and one test (#guard). Made by a program. No Mathlib.
The files are proof files of the first toy, a test protocol for the claim that three rows a, b, c of
one oracle message have a_i b_i = c_i for every i (the heads of Fmt/M1Types.lean and
Fmt/M1Psc.lean).

Must-refuse test data of a toy: made-up data only, no model and no weights. The files are this
library's own, not the programs' (the programs: the C++ implementation that comes with this Lean
code). They were made by this library's own reference program in Python, at small sizes of this
library's own. The head of
Fmt/M1FilesB.lean gives the sizes, and that file has the set's honest file (`honest`).

The kinds of bad files.
  cheat: one message is altered before its query; all that follows is made by the honest rules.
  false: a false statement, argued by the honest rules.
  word: no code word. One word of a column is altered before the tree is built.
  recut: a nonce that fails the work test; all that follows is made by the honest rules.
A verifier that accepts one is broken. These files are test fixtures: proofs that every verifier
must refuse. Test data: every file here is false, or breaks a rule of the protocol, and must be
refused.

The words.
  query: in each round the prover's message is hashed together with the chain's value so far.
  a query's answer: it gives the chain's next value and the verifier's message (the challenges).
  work test: the test of the proof of work.
  PSC, CS, BASE, in the names of messages and rounds: PSC is the sum-check, CS the code switch and
    BASE the last block (the head of Fmt/M1Dec.lean).
  FOLD: a sum-check that folds a linear claim (Fmt/M1Fold.lean).

Main names:
  `Dn`: the five parts of the decision. d1 is form, d2 proof of work, d3 Merkle openings, d4 the
    last block's linear check, d5 its checks at the drawn positions. How the decision is divided:
    the head of Fmt/M1Types.lean.
  `mustBeRefused`: name, bytes, the expected refusal: the part at which the verifier of this
  library's own reference
    refuses it.

Limits: this file does not say what any verifier in Lean says of them. For tests on files of the set
see Fmt/M1PlanKnownB.lean (the strict reader) and Fmt/M1Tiny.lean (the whole verifier on bytes).

Terms: lean/GLOSSARY.md.
-/
